-- title:
--   Lemma 9, p.20 — boundedness of the outer iterates
-- statement:
--   Assume the standing assumptions of §3: $\varepsilon>0$, $\gamma_0>0$, $z^0\in\mathrm{dom}\,B$, $0<\delta<1$, $0<\nu\le1/2$, $\eta\in[0,\nu/(1+\nu))$, $\rho_0\ge1$, $0<\tau_0\le1$, $\zeta>1$, $0<\sigma<1/\zeta$, Assumption 1 with $\mu=0$, and let $x^*$ be an arbitrary fixed solution of (1). For a run of Algorithm 2,
--   $$
--   \|z^p-x^*\|\le\|z^0-x^*\|+\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}\rho_k\tau_k\quad\text{for every generated }z^p,
--   $$
--   $$
--   \|z^{p+1}-z^p\|\le\|z^0-x^*\|+\sum_{k=0}^{p}\rho_k\tau_k\quad\text{for every completed outer iteration }p.
--   $$
--
--   Because $\sum_k\rho_k\tau_k=\Lambda<\infty$, all subproblems live in one bounded region, and $\|z^{k+1}-z^k\|/\rho_k\to0$ forces the outer test (15).
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: $F$ is a point-valued operator on $\mathrm{cl}(\mathrm{dom}\,B)$ → a total map on $\mathbb R^n$, constrained only on $\mathrm{cl}(\mathrm{dom}\,B)$; reason: values of $F$ elsewhere never enter $F+B$ or a run, since iterates lie in $\mathrm{dom}\,B$
--   - encoding: Assumption 1(d), the resolvent of $\gamma B$ can be exactly evaluated → a family $J$ with $J_\gamma=(I+\gamma B)^{-1}$ for every $\gamma>0$; reason: the assumption has no mathematical content; the family exists and is unique on $\gamma>0$
--   - encoding: iterates generated by Algorithm 2, $0\le k\in\mathbb K-1$ → the prefix predicate `Alg2.IsPrefix` at index $s$, with $k<s$; reason: $k\in\mathbb K-1$ says exactly that $z^{k+1}$ has been generated
--   - generalization: (70) and (71) for $s\ge1$ → also at index $0$; reason: (70) is trivial there and the printed proof of (71) goes through unchanged; the proof of Theorem 3 uses (71) at $s=K$, and $K=0$ occurs; the printed range is a special case
-- source:
--   Lu & Mei, Primal-dual extrapolation methods for monotone inclusions under local Lipschitz continuity, arXiv:2206.00973v3, Lemma 9, p.20

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ThreeOpSplitting_Convergence_MonotoneOperators
import Definitions.Def_MonoIncExtrap_Setting
import Definitions.Def_MonoIncExtrap_Algorithm1
import Definitions.Def_MonoIncExtrap_Algorithm2

open InnerProductSpace ThreeOpSplitting.Convergence

namespace MonoIncExtrap

/-- **Lemma 9.** arXiv:2206.00973v3, Lemma 9, p.20. For a run of Algorithm 2,
`‖z^p − x∗‖ ≤ ‖z⁰ − x∗‖ + Σ_{k=0}^{p−1} ρ_kτ_k` (70) and
`‖z^{p+1} − z^p‖ ≤ ‖z⁰ − x∗‖ + Σ_{k=0}^{p} ρ_kτ_k` (71).

**Formalization Note.** The page prints both for `p ≥ 1`. Here `p = 0` is included: (70) is then
trivial, and the proof of (71) goes through unchanged; the proof of Theorem 3 uses (71) at `p = K`,
and `K = 0` occurs. -/
theorem lemma_9 {n : ℕ}
    {B : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n) → Set (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))}
    {I : Alg2.Input (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n))} {xstar : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)}
    (hI : Alg2.Standing B I xstar)
    {z : ℕ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n)} {c : ℕ → ℕ} {s : ℕ}
    (hz : Alg2.IsPrefix I z c s) :
    (∀ p ≤ s, ‖z p - xstar‖ ≤ ‖z 0 - xstar‖ + ∑ k ∈ Finset.range p, I.rho k * I.tau k) ∧
    (∀ p < s, ‖z (p + 1) - z p‖ ≤
      ‖z 0 - xstar‖ + ∑ k ∈ Finset.range (p + 1), I.rho k * I.tau k) := by sorry

end MonoIncExtrap
